# Website Access Denial Script

Here's a simple script that can be used to deny access to a website or service. This example uses JavaScript for client-side denial, but note that proper access control should be implemented server-side for security.

## Client-Side JavaScript Example (for demonstration purposes)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Access Denied</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            background-color: #f8f9fa;
            color: #dc3545;
            text-align: center;
            padding: 50px;
        }
        .denied-container {
            max-width: 600px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 20px;
            border: 1px solid #dc3545;
            border-radius: 5px;
            background-color: #fff;
        }
        h1 {
            color: #dc3545;
        }
        .icon {
            font-size: 48px;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="denied-container">
        <div class="icon">⛔</div>
        <h1>Access Denied</h1>
        <p>You do not have permission to access this website or service.</p>
        <p>If you believe this is an error, please contact the administrator.</p>
        <p id="redirect-message"></p>
    </div>

    <script>
        // Redirect after delay
        let countdown = 10;
        const redirectMessage = document.getElementById('redirect-message');
        redirectMessage.textContent = `You will be redirected to the homepage in ${countdown} seconds.`;

        const countdownInterval = setInterval(() => {
            countdown--;
            redirectMessage.textContent = `You will be redirected to the homepage in ${countdown} seconds.`;

            if (countdown <= 0) {
                clearInterval(countdownInterval);
                window.location.href = 'https://example.com'; // Replace with your homepage
            }
        }, 1000);

        // Alternative: Close the window/tab
        // setTimeout(() => { window.close(); }, 10000);
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

## Server-Side Examples

For actual implementation, server-side controls are much more secure:

### PHP Example
```php
<?php
// Check for some access condition (e.g., user role, IP address, etc.)
$hasAccess = false; // Replace with your actual access check logic

if (!$hasAccess) {
    header('HTTP/1.0 403 Forbidden');
    die('Access Denied. You do not have permission to access this resource.');
}

// Rest of your code for authorized users
?>
```

### Node.js/Express Example
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use((req, res, next) => {
    // Replace with your actual access check logic
    const hasAccess = false;

    if (!hasAccess) {
        return res.status(403).send('Access Denied');
    }
    next();
});

// Routes for authorized users
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
    res.send('Welcome to the protected content');
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});
```

### Python Flask Example
```python
from flask import Flask, abort

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.before_request
def check_access():
    # Replace with your actual access check logic
    has_access = False

    if not has_access:
        abort(403, description="Access Denied")

@app.route('/')
def index():
    return "Welcome to the protected content"

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run()
```

## Important Notes:
1. Client-side scripts can be bypassed, so always implement proper server-side authentication and authorization.
2. For production use, implement proper authentication systems (OAuth, JWT, session-based auth, etc.).
3. Consider logging access attempts for security monitoring.
4. Provide clear but non-technical error messages to unauthorized users.
